and Cloud Spanner.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:12.620
Today I'm going to talk about
how customers are future

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.920
proofing their business for
global scale and consistency

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.900
with Cloud Spanner.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.450
So as part of this
talk I'm going

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:22.440
to cover three main areas.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.820
First and foremost, I'm going to
introduce you to Cloud Spanner.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:29.210
Second, we are seeing
a lot of momentum

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:32.070
with Cloud Spanner
for our customers.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:35.330
And I'm going to talk
about what customers

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:38.090
are using Cloud Spanner,
which verticals,

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:39.770
and what other use cases.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:44.750
And last, I'm going to share
some recent developments which

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.640
we have done in Cloud Spanner.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.470
New features, new capabilities,
which are up and coming.

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.780
So we'll talk about that.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:57.150
So to get to it Cloud
Spanner is a unique database.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.960
Basically, Cloud Spanner
offers horizontal scalability

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.240
with complete
relational semantics.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:04.860
Horizontal scale
is something which

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:09.000
you would expect from
non-relational databases, which

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.290
are sharded and everything.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:13.560
Spanner does all that, in
addition to offering you

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:16.430
asset transactions,
complete sequence support,

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:18.750
all the relational
semantics which you expect

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.760
from a relational database.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.820
To top it off it's
highly available,

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.265
means we are talking about
five-nines of availability

00:01:26.265 --> 00:01:28.660
in a multi-regional
configuration.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:30.190
It's fully managed.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:34.260
And this is something which
Google built for itself.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.950
A number of our billion
plus user products

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:38.840
are sitting on top of Spanner.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.905
They use Spanner as
the main database.

00:01:40.905 --> 00:01:42.780
And it's something
which we have been

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.940
using for quite a long time.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:47.810
Now how do we make it work?

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:50.830
What are the key building
blocks for Spanner?

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:53.210
So there are three
main elements to it.

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.930
First and foremost, it's
the Google's network.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:02:01.630
So Spanner is synchronously
replicated behind the scenes.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:05.470
So network plays a
large role as part

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:10.030
of what we offer to our
customers in terms of latency,

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:11.680
in terms of reliability.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.110
And having Google's network
infrastructure backing Cloud

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:17.660
Spanner is really important.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.890
And as you know Google's
network infrastructure

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:22.900
is redundant highly available.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.320
And Spanner being
multi-regional and something

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.100
which our customers are using
as a global database network

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.630
plays a big role.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:32.960
Second is True Time.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.860
So the picture on the right
of the screen as you can see,

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:40.330
they are atomic oscillators
and GPS antennas.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.220
This is actually
a picture for one

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:45.550
of our servers in
our data centers.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:48.250
And basically what
it's showing you is we

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.730
have atomic and GPD
clocks in our data center.

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:55.000
And what it offers
is to our customers,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.440
this gives a global
notion of time for Spanner

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:00.640
to function on.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.660
So once there is a global
notion of time for a distributed

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:08.860
database we can promise
external strong consistency

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:10.520
to our customers.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.770
And this is something which
is extremely important,

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:16.310
the ordering of transactions
for financial services,

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:18.075
for retail sector.

00:03:18.075 --> 00:03:19.700
And that is something
which we are only

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:21.490
able to offer
because of True Time.

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:25.410
And last night but not least, we
have a very optimized software

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:28.180
stack with a custom
Paxos implementation,

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:31.450
automatic resharding based
on load and size of data.

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:35.950
All the magic which
makes Spanner work.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:39.240
So now naturally a
question needs to be asked,

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.170
why Cloud Spanner?

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:43.980
What is that's so
different in comparison

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:45.480
to a relational database?

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.230
First and foremost, as I
already talked about earlier,

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:49.020
it's scale.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:52.200
And the scale insurance
which our customers get out

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.880
of Spanner with
high availability.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:59.160
So in terms of scale there
is literally no limit.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:02.370
And you can start small
and seamlessly scale.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:04.200
That is very
important for a number

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.200
of use cases, which we'll
talk about in the later part

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.940
of the presentation.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.580
Second, multi-region with
transparent synchronous

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:12.420
replication.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.700
We are talking about
a one click experience

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:20.459
in setting up a global instance
across multiple continents.

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:22.580
Strong external
consistency, which I already

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:23.700
discussed about.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:30.240
Pretty much as one walks the
relational database experience

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:34.320
you can get from a horizontally
scalable Cloud Spanner

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.020
database.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:40.260
There's no concept of
planned maintenance windows.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.150
And that's why we
are able to offer you

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:44.110
such high availability.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:47.430
It's completely managed, you
can all also do completely

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.170
online schema changes.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:52.290
You don't have to take
a down time for that.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:55.200
We have complete separation
of storage and compute.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:59.140
This becomes extremely
important for scalability.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:04.440
So you can seamlessly scale
your workload because there's

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:06.340
no coupling between
storage and compute,

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.910
and you can scale
them independently.

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:11.460
We launch manage backups, I'm
going to talk a little bit more

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.610
in detail about it, but
there are consistent backups.

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:17.070
We also offer cell level
concurrency control

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:20.520
which is extremely important for
a database like Spanner which

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.780
has use cases where
customers are wishing

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:24.690
and millions of QPS.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:27.750
And cell level concurrency
control really helps.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:32.880
And again, we have databases
internally which have

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:37.420
which are petabytes plus
size for a single database.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:43.050
So scale both on the QPS side
as well as the storage side.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:47.030
So this is just a
representation of some

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:48.500
of the key feature highlights.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:50.810
Again, I'm not going to
talk about each one of them.

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:52.850
But these are the
different categories

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:54.980
where we continue to
innovate and offer

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:58.350
more features and
capabilities to our customers.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.320
First and foremost,
it's the enterprise

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:01.550
and relational capabilities.

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:05.210
So I talked about fine
grained concurrency control,

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:08.330
manage backups, we
have secondary indexes.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:10.340
On the programmability
side, we continue

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:12.830
to add more client drivers.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:16.181
We have eight
which are now in G.

00:06:16.181 --> 00:06:18.920
We have complete DDL
and DMLs onboarding,

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:20.840
including Batch DML.

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:23.540
We have commit timestamps,
which customers are using it

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:25.850
for multiple use cases,
foreign keys which

00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:27.770
was recently launched.

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:31.010
Local Emulator, we'll
talk a lot about some

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:33.450
of these capabilities
later on as well.

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:39.320
And then on the security side
we launched VPC Service Control,

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:41.780
Access Transparency.

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:45.290
All the data by default is
encrypted-at-rest, that's

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:47.060
something which we offer.

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:49.190
So a lot of capabilities
which customers

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:52.710
expect from enterprise database
from a security standpoint.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:59.270
And which not just Spanner, but
GCP overall is very good at.

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:02.240
In terms of monitoring
and introspection

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:04.280
we have a lot of
capabilities which

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:10.160
we continue to offer with more
so we can provide more of you

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:12.860
into what your
workload is doing.

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:16.520
We have open cenus integration,
CPU utilization graph,

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:20.470
latency graphs, a
number of those things.

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:24.640
So moving on, now
I talked about what

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:28.550
is Cloud Spanner, what are some
of the key capabilities which

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:29.560
it offers.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:31.960
I think it's important to
understand why customers

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:33.430
are choosing Cloud Spanner.

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:37.210
And for that it's
important to know

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:40.570
what are some of the challenges
which customers face today.

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:44.650
And how Spanner kind of plays
a role in terms of alleviating

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:46.810
some of those challenges.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:49.740
So first and foremost, there
are these business challenges

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:50.640
for customers.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:54.810
For example, the first
one is the capital expense

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:59.470
which they have to take for
some seasonal peak workloads.

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:01.440
So let's say you
are a retailer, you

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:04.350
are planning for your couple
of days of Black Friday,

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:05.910
Cyber Monday sale.

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:08.670
And to pay for all that
fixed infrastructure

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:11.010
for just those couple of days
where you might experience

00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:13.990
1,000% scaling is quite a bit.

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:19.410
So that is something which goes
on on the books and something

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:22.230
we are concerned about.

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:25.730
Then the second thing
is, all our customers,

00:08:25.730 --> 00:08:28.170
majority of them, they
have global customer base

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:29.680
of their own.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:32.346
They want to provide a great
experience to their customers

00:08:32.346 --> 00:08:34.679
so they have to invest in
multi-regional presence, which

00:08:34.679 --> 00:08:36.120
is again not cheap.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:39.130
And last but not least is
the operational expense,

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:43.260
which is exacerbated by
some of the seasonal scaling

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:45.550
which comes with the workloads.

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:48.390
So you have to plan for that
operation and maintenance

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:49.620
and everything.

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:52.590
So all this ties
into the expense,

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:55.140
but there is a human element
to it as well because

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:58.040
of operations and maintenance.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:02.150
On the technical side a number
of customers we work with

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:04.820
are very concerned
about high availability.

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:09.260
In today's day and age, if
you are serving your traffic

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:12.180
at the peak time, whether
it's health care for health

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:15.245
care, its enrollment
season, for retailers

00:09:15.245 --> 00:09:21.680
it's some of those big days,
and for financial services

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:25.680
any other times when a lot
of payments are being made.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:28.430
High business continuity
is needed, so how

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:32.390
do you plan for that with
appropriate DR capabilities.

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:34.910
There should be
absolutely no downtime.

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:36.890
It should be able
to scale seamlessly

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:38.480
based on the workload.

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:42.120
Consistency is
extremely important.

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:44.630
For example, if you are
doing a debit and credit

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:47.960
in financial application
it's extremely important

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:50.870
that the ordering of
those transactions

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:55.740
is maintained by the database
as part of when it was done.

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:58.520
And if you go back and
take a look that order

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:00.470
should remain the same.

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:04.550
And again, the global
nature of the world which

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:07.940
we live in from a customer
base perspective, everybody

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:12.320
wants to serve their
customers with low latency,

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:15.420
and they need a consistent
view of data globally.

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:18.410
And last but not least, the
solution which they have

00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:20.870
needs to integrate
with multiple systems.

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:23.960
And whether a database
provides those hooks.

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:26.000
So based on the
critical capabilities

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:27.560
which we discussed
earlier, Spanner

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:31.820
fits the bill in all of this,
and meets the requirements

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:35.170
which customers come to us.

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:39.780
So as a nation we are seeing
a lot of momentum on Spanner

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:42.880
across multiple verticals.

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:45.070
Some of them are listed here.

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:50.130
And if we think from basically
the differentiators of Spanner,

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:54.570
which is extreme scaling,
higher availability,

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:59.080
strong consistency of
data, and ease of use,

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:01.110
it's really easy to
understand why all these use

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:02.520
cases are coming to Spanner.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:06.180
Across gaming, financial
services, health care,

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:09.330
media entertainment, technology,
a lot of technology companies

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:12.900
are coming and adopting
Spanner in retail.

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:17.310
So let's just picking up on
now from the gaming side,

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:20.850
if you are a game developer,
and you are making

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:23.350
a game you like to start small.

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:29.160
And once the game makes it big
or you are the next Pokemon Go,

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:32.020
then you don't have
time to re-architect

00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:33.370
the app for that scale.

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:37.790
So Spanner allows you
to do exactly that.

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:41.040
That you can start small
with the right schema,

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:42.720
and then you can
seamlessly scale

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:46.380
from hundreds to thousands
to millions of queries

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:48.560
per second in transactions.

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:51.840
And for data size
which can be similarly

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:56.160
like in megabytes,
GBs or even petabytes.

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:57.720
And a lot of
customers are using it

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:01.530
to store user profile,
real time gameplay data,

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:03.600
and a lot of those
applications are there.

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:05.820
Similarly, on
financial services,

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:10.470
extreme high availability,
external consistency,

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:11.160
all those.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:15.070
And definitely ability to
scale is extremely important.

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:19.110
And that's why a
number of banks are now

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:22.500
switching to Spanner for the
online banking and ledger use

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:23.490
cases.

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:27.060
Health care, we have a number
of big health care customers

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:29.760
who are implementing
the complete EMR

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:33.540
systems on Spanner, which
is extremely exciting for us

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.580
to see the health care space.

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:41.310
With all the compliance, HIPAA,
PCI, we are in compliance,

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:44.140
we offer that kind of
compliance to our customers.

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:46.680
So as long as it's
implemented correctly,

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:50.460
so health care and across a lot
of these regulatory industries

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:53.570
we are seeing a lot of
adoption, detailed technology.

00:12:53.570 --> 00:12:55.980
So as a next step,
I'm not going to dive

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:59.400
into each of these use cases,
but just pick a couple of them

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:02.180
and discuss them in detail.

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:05.680
So first off, on this
is financial services

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:08.380
retail banking use case.

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:11.930
This slide presents the complete
solution architecture for it.

00:13:11.930 --> 00:13:14.860
So as you can see,
basically the use cases

00:13:14.860 --> 00:13:18.580
there's this
traditional retail bank

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:22.180
which has migrated to Spanner.

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:26.500
In this day in age they
have omni-channel way

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:30.000
for their customers to access
the same financial data

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.940
and transact with that
through mobile, through web,

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:35.180
through voice, and
even third party.

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:39.340
And this is basically just
shown at the top of the slide.

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:41.890
And then Spanner
piece is right there

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:46.360
in this red rectangle which is
