which we have seen where a
Spanner connects with GKE.

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:57.200
And so it can seamlessly scale
both from the client side,

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:58.810
as well as on the database side.

00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:02.110
So it's a very typical pattern.

00:14:02.110 --> 00:14:04.510
And then as I was
mentioning earlier

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:07.600
it's extremely important
that the database

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:11.120
can hook up with the legacy
systems or other systems which

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:11.620
are there.

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:14.282
For example, the other
core banking systems

00:14:14.282 --> 00:14:15.740
which are there or
payment gateways

00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:17.650
as is shown at the bottom.

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:20.890
And then as the data
is getting updated

00:14:20.890 --> 00:14:24.700
in the OLTP database,
which is Spanner,

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:26.680
the data almost
in near real time

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:29.620
is getting populated
in analytics

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:31.610
and warehousing
solutions as well.

00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:35.157
So the customer is able to
do that [INAUDIBLE] queries

00:14:35.157 --> 00:14:36.490
and generate insights from that.

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:39.882
That is within BigQuery
and Cloud AI data

00:14:39.882 --> 00:14:40.840
catalog and everything.

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:47.230
So that whole chain is set up
using Pub/Sub and Dataflow.

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:49.220
And on the security
and governance side,

00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:52.390
we offer all kinds
of integration,

00:14:52.390 --> 00:14:55.150
like Cloud Monitoring
DLP and customers

00:14:55.150 --> 00:14:59.690
are able to benefit with
that across the board.

00:14:59.690 --> 00:15:02.680
So this is just one of
those solution architectures

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:05.890
where Spanner plays that
operational database role

00:15:05.890 --> 00:15:08.440
and is able to
seamlessly scale along

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:10.600
with the rest of the
architecture, which

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:14.560
is extremely critical
for our customers.

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:18.630
Next I'm not talking about the
complete retail omni-channel

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:20.740
retail architecture
for this customer,

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:22.960
but just focusing on
the Spanner piece.

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:27.250
So in this case the use case
was the omni-channel retail

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:31.540
customer is getting
updated prices delivered

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:34.160
to GCP through an
external system,

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:36.310
both as a streaming
data pipeline

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:40.150
as well as in batches the
data was being uploaded.

00:15:40.150 --> 00:15:42.790
And then Spanenr
was the database

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:47.080
of record for all the SKUs
with the updated prices.

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:49.690
And then Spanner is
serving those prices

00:15:49.690 --> 00:15:54.460
to all the applications which
this omni-channel retailer is

00:15:54.460 --> 00:15:55.240
running.

00:15:55.240 --> 00:15:57.910
So as you can see from
the solution architecture

00:15:57.910 --> 00:16:01.850
that the prices are being
uploaded in cloud storage.

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:06.810
And then through load service
running as part of Kubernetes

00:16:06.810 --> 00:16:09.610
is being uploaded
into Cloud Spanner.

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:13.120
And then Cloud Spanner is
just serving all the requests

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:15.220
from the pricing API standpoint.

00:16:15.220 --> 00:16:17.920
Again, in this case
Spanner is getting

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:20.620
a lot of write load
as well as read load.

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:23.290
And depending on
the traffic it's

00:16:23.290 --> 00:16:27.040
able to scale up based on
the needs of the application

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:29.510
and the [INAUDIBLE]
and everything.

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:31.995
So again, very important.

00:16:31.995 --> 00:16:33.370
And in this case
that we're using

00:16:33.370 --> 00:16:37.330
a multi-regional instance,
so five-nines of availability

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:40.000
with global presence
and everything.

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:43.780
So extremely happy with
the way it turned out

00:16:43.780 --> 00:16:49.400
and the customer is really
very happy with the solution.

00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:51.820
So switching gears now,
let's talk about what's

00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:54.740
new in Cloud Spanner.

00:16:54.740 --> 00:16:59.150
And just a reflection of
the past a little bit.

00:16:59.150 --> 00:17:01.870
So we had a busy 2019.

00:17:01.870 --> 00:17:05.640
We delivered capabilities
and three of--

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:07.930
in all areas basically.

00:17:07.930 --> 00:17:11.460
We have categorized in these
three major areas, enterprise

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:14.589
capabilities developer
productivity, and SQL.

00:17:14.589 --> 00:17:16.690
So on the enterprise
side I already talked

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:19.480
about VPC access
transparency, which

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:24.220
is extremely critical for some
of our regulatory industry

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:25.140
users.

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:27.730
Where when even
Google S.R.D. when

00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:32.980
they touched your data for any
reason, that kind of an action

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:34.120
has to be locked.

00:17:34.120 --> 00:17:35.890
So that is access transparency.

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:39.250
Spanner supports
access transparency.

00:17:39.250 --> 00:17:44.150
And we updated our SLA
in September, 2019.

00:17:44.150 --> 00:17:48.230
Basically now all your
Cloud Spanner instances,

00:17:48.230 --> 00:17:49.960
even with a single
node, are powered

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:51.940
under the same production SLA.

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:55.660
So this way even for
smaller workloads

00:17:55.660 --> 00:18:00.100
you are protected by the
same SLA which we offer.

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:04.000
Before September, 2019 we used
to require a minimum three

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:05.570
nodes in an instance.

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:07.555
And the reason we were
able to update our SLA

00:18:07.555 --> 00:18:10.450
is because Spanner behind
the scenes is replicated.

00:18:10.450 --> 00:18:12.310
So even in case of
a regional instance

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:13.780
there are three replicas.

00:18:13.780 --> 00:18:16.810
And we are able to offer
you that high availability

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:18.520
with a single node.

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:22.000
And multi-region has
multiple replicas,

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:24.310
like five, seven, and nine
replica configurations is

00:18:24.310 --> 00:18:26.130
something which we offer today.

00:18:26.130 --> 00:18:27.980
On the developer
productivity side,

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:29.950
we continue to invest in ORMs.

00:18:29.950 --> 00:18:32.920
We have launched a new
initiative and something

00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:35.470
which is getting a lot of
momentum called Cloud Spanner

00:18:35.470 --> 00:18:36.168
Ecosystem.

00:18:36.168 --> 00:18:38.210
The website is actually
cloudspannerecosystem.dev

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:40.570
or DEV.

00:18:40.570 --> 00:18:43.660
And because as you
noticed, we have

00:18:43.660 --> 00:18:45.460
a lot of momentum with Spanner.

00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:48.610
A number of customers
are contributing tools

00:18:48.610 --> 00:18:52.450
in the ecosystem for
other Spanner users.

00:18:52.450 --> 00:18:56.260
And we have a lot of
partners contributing tools

00:18:56.260 --> 00:18:58.030
and Google is as well.

00:18:58.030 --> 00:19:01.120
So we created this
ecosystem as a single place

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:03.160
where they can make
their contributions

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:05.140
for the benefit of
the overall community,

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:06.670
and we are seeing
a lot of traction.

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:08.025
Please check it out.

00:19:08.025 --> 00:19:09.400
There are a number
of tools which

00:19:09.400 --> 00:19:11.650
are available for our users.

00:19:11.650 --> 00:19:14.710
And last but not the
least on the SQL side, we

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:19.030
continue to innovate for
the query optimizer relaunch

00:19:19.030 --> 00:19:24.070
cost based index selection as
part of the query optimization.

00:19:24.070 --> 00:19:26.950
We have Batch DML support,
we launch WITH clause.

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:30.250
Again, trying to offer
all the capabilities which

00:19:30.250 --> 00:19:36.990
you expect from a database
from the SQL side.

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:42.060
We had a fairly busy 2020 so far
again in all those three areas

00:19:42.060 --> 00:19:47.070
we continue to make improvements
and launch new capabilities.

00:19:47.070 --> 00:19:49.793
And manage
backup-restore, I'm not

00:19:49.793 --> 00:19:51.210
going to spend too
much time here.

00:19:51.210 --> 00:19:53.670
I'll go into details later.

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:56.610
We continue to expand on our
multi-regional configuration

00:19:56.610 --> 00:19:58.700
options.

00:19:58.700 --> 00:20:02.790
We originally had four
multi-regional instance

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:04.020
configurations.

00:20:04.020 --> 00:20:08.220
We recently launched two new
multi-regional configurations.

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:12.070
One in Asia only, [INAUDIBLE]
and another one in Europe.

00:20:12.070 --> 00:20:16.330
And then we recently launched
a change watcher solution,

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:19.080
which is not a native
capability but a solution, which

00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:23.160
is built on top of Spanner
leveraging the current times

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:25.170
feature, where
customers can watch out

00:20:25.170 --> 00:20:28.730
for changes which are happening
within their database.

00:20:28.730 --> 00:20:30.780
And on the developer
productivity side

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:34.610
we-- a emulator is something,
this is an offline emulator.

00:20:34.610 --> 00:20:36.810
I'm going to talk
more in details later.

00:20:36.810 --> 00:20:40.500
But C++ Client library,
Transaction Statistics,

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:44.910
and Harbor Bridge which is
another tool which was recently

00:20:44.910 --> 00:20:47.700
contributed in Cloud
Spanner Ecosystem.

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:51.960
It is to aid in migration.

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:54.900
It actually, the
capability it provides

00:20:54.900 --> 00:20:59.760
is if a customer has
a PostgreSQL schema

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:02.520
and they use Harbor
Bridge, the tool

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:05.740
can recommend an equal
Spanner schema with it.

00:21:05.740 --> 00:21:09.020
So originally we launched
it for PostgreSQL support,

00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:14.280
and for MySQL we have
launched it in beta right now.

00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:16.560
And on the SQL side Query
Optimizer Versioning,

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:20.680
I talk more in details on
that later, and Foreign Keys.

00:21:20.680 --> 00:21:22.240
So I'm not going
to go into details

00:21:22.240 --> 00:21:24.910
of each one of
these capabilities,

00:21:24.910 --> 00:21:27.220
but I'll pick one from
each of these areas

00:21:27.220 --> 00:21:30.620
and tell you a little
bit more about them.

00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:33.290
So first and foremost
is managed backups,

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:39.260
which we launched with
general availability in April.

00:21:39.260 --> 00:21:41.740
So this is on demand
managed backups.

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:46.300
This is primarily to offer
protection to our customers

00:21:46.300 --> 00:21:49.210
against operator
application errors which can

00:21:49.210 --> 00:21:51.410
cause logical data corruption.

00:21:51.410 --> 00:21:54.310
So what we offer is
consistent backup

00:21:54.310 --> 00:21:57.490
for regional and
multi-regional instances.

00:21:57.490 --> 00:22:03.010
And what we are optimizing
for is extremely fast restores

00:22:03.010 --> 00:22:05.710
or time to first-byte access.

00:22:05.710 --> 00:22:08.080
Because if you run
into an issue you

00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:10.810
would like to get back
online with your application

00:22:10.810 --> 00:22:13.340
as soon as possible,
and we recognize that.

00:22:13.340 --> 00:22:18.760
So even for a backup,
which is terabytes in size,

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:21.520
you can expect to
get first-byte access

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:24.470
as part of your restore
process within minutes.

00:22:24.470 --> 00:22:27.715
So that is something which
we have really optimized for.

00:22:27.715 --> 00:22:30.790
You can restore from your
backup within the same instance

00:22:30.790 --> 00:22:36.070
configuration or basically
within the same instance

00:22:36.070 --> 00:22:39.250
or another instance within the
same instance configuration.

00:22:39.250 --> 00:22:42.610
And the maximum retention
time for backups is one year.

00:22:42.610 --> 00:22:46.840
And from a security
perspective, we

00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:50.210
are IM access permissions
are separate for backup.

00:22:50.210 --> 00:22:53.350
So customers have
the flexibility

00:22:53.350 --> 00:22:57.580
to create a separate role
just to manage the backups

00:22:57.580 --> 00:22:59.800
and do the restores.

00:22:59.800 --> 00:23:01.130
So that is there.

00:23:01.130 --> 00:23:04.700
And again, the feature is
available through API, Console,

00:23:04.700 --> 00:23:06.770
gCloud.

00:23:06.770 --> 00:23:09.390
Local Emulator, so
this is something which

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:13.390
we announced in beta in March.

00:23:13.390 --> 00:23:16.950
I'm happy to announce the
general availability of Local

00:23:16.950 --> 00:23:19.870
Emulator as part of this talk.

00:23:19.870 --> 00:23:21.930
So this is now
generally available.

00:23:21.930 --> 00:23:27.990
We have implemented a
lot more capabilities

00:23:27.990 --> 00:23:30.870
as part of this general
available launch for Local

00:23:30.870 --> 00:23:31.770
Emulator.

00:23:31.770 --> 00:23:35.130
It is an offline open
source local emulator.

00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:37.240
The code is open,
you can take a look.

00:23:37.240 --> 00:23:39.790
It's in our GitHub repository.

00:23:39.790 --> 00:23:44.160
It offers complete REST
and gRPC API support.

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:48.990
And it offers SQL, basically
DML and DDL support.

00:23:48.990 --> 00:23:50.820
Most importantly, it
has complete support

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:52.750
for information schema as well.

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:54.840
Now, all client
libraries are supported

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:56.400
for working with Emulator.

00:23:56.400 --> 00:23:58.740
And this really, we
have got great feedback

00:23:58.740 --> 00:24:00.280
from a number of our customers.

00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:04.290
And it has really helped them
from productivity, developer

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:06.370
productivity perspective,
because now they

00:24:06.370 --> 00:24:11.460
can do a very quick correctness
testing for their application

00:24:11.460 --> 00:24:14.510
in an offline environment.

00:24:14.510 --> 00:24:18.520
And last but not the
least, Cloud Spanner

00:24:18.520 --> 00:24:21.400
offered Query Optimizer
Versioning capability

00:24:21.400 --> 00:24:23.330
to our customers.

00:24:23.330 --> 00:24:25.580
And this is a GA feature.

00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:28.210
So basically what does it mean?

00:24:28.210 --> 00:24:30.820
Now users have the flexibility
to run their queries

00:24:30.820 --> 00:24:34.210
with a specific version
of Query Optimizer.

00:24:34.210 --> 00:24:36.840
There's a range of
choices which we offer.

00:24:36.840 --> 00:24:39.160
You can pin your
complete database

00:24:39.160 --> 00:24:43.150
on a query optimize
version, or you

00:24:43.150 --> 00:24:45.880
can decide to pin
just your application

00:24:45.880 --> 00:24:50.230
on a specific query optimizer
version with the default query

00:24:50.230 --> 00:24:53.000
optimizer for the database
as being something different.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:56.180
So you can override the
default on the database.

00:24:56.180 --> 00:25:00.430
And finally, you can use
a specific query optimizer

00:25:00.430 --> 00:25:02.870
origin as a query hint as well.

00:25:02.870 --> 00:25:05.720
And you can test your query
with a specific query optimizer

00:25:05.720 --> 00:25:06.560
version.

00:25:06.560 --> 00:25:09.140
So there's a range of
choices and flexibility

00:25:09.140 --> 00:25:11.200
which we offer as
part of this feature.

00:25:11.200 --> 00:25:15.330
And customers can decide
to pin their application

00:25:15.330 --> 00:25:19.600
the whole database and get that
same predictable performance

00:25:19.600 --> 00:25:21.830
from Spanner.

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:25.890
So that concludes my
presentation, but as next steps

00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:30.150
we have a couple of very
interesting talks planned

00:25:30.150 --> 00:25:31.460
for Spanner.

00:25:31.460 --> 00:25:36.390
First, we have our talk
which one of our customers

00:25:36.390 --> 00:25:41.090
is going to give about switching
from DynamoDB to Cloud Bigtable

00:25:41.090 --> 00:25:41.760
and Spanner.

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:43.710
Please tune into that.

00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:48.750
It was a fairly big undertaking
in terms of migration

00:25:48.750 --> 00:25:50.040
from Dynamo.

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:53.490
You're talking terabytes
of data and a lot

00:25:53.490 --> 00:25:55.610
of throughput in
that application

00:25:55.610 --> 00:25:59.250
was then migrated from Dynamo
onto Spanner and Bigtable.

00:25:59.250 --> 00:26:00.750
And then there's
another talk, which

00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:04.690
one of our gaming specialists
